{"gid":"2026_01_ATL_PIT","week":1,"away":"ATL","home":"PIT","away_col":"#FF525B","home_col":"#FFD84D","spread":3.0,"total":42.5,"fav":"PIT","hpts":23,"apts":20,"kickoff":"Sun Sep 13 \u00b7 13:00","roof":"Outdoors","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Drake London</strong> draws a plus matchup \u2014 PIT concede <strong>67 yds/game to WR1s</strong> (league ~58), and ATL funnel 26% of targets his way. A rec-yards spot.</p><p>The <strong>PIT</strong> host the <strong>ATL</strong> as favorites of <strong>3.0</strong>, with the total set at <strong>42.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>PIT 23\u201320</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>31% of its snaps with PIT in front</strong>, <strong>41% within one score</strong>, and <strong>27% with PIT chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>This is a <strong>coin-flip script</strong>. With only 3.0 points separating them, neither team projects to spend much time in a blowout state \u2014 most of the game lives in the 'close' bucket, where play-calling stays balanced and usage tracks each player's established role rather than being distorted by score. In tight games the stars on both sides see their normal, dependable workloads, and there is less garbage-time leakage to backups.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p><strong>Tua Tagovailoa</strong> has a passing-yards tailwind \u2014 PIT allow <strong>250 pass yds/game</strong> (vs ~230).</p><p>Sneaky value on <strong>Jahan Dotson</strong> (WR2) \u2014 PIT leak 42 yds/game to the WR2 role, above the ~36 norm.</p><p><strong>DK Metcalf</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 ATL hold WR1s to just 52 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Kyle Pitts</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 PIT are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.39 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p><strong>DK Metcalf</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 ATL surrender 0.47 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><h3>PIT \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>PIT are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (42% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Jaylen Warren</strong> (13 carries, 58 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>DK Metcalf</strong> projects for <strong>7 targets and 58 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Pat Freiermuth</strong> (TE, 4 targets, 34 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>ATL \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>ATL are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>33%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p><strong>Bijan Robinson</strong> (15 carries, 72 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>Drake London</strong> projects for <strong>7 targets and 53 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Kyle Pitts</strong> (TE, 8 targets, 66 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 3-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"ATL","col":"#FF525B","players":[{"name":"Tua Tagovailoa","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":208.54,"book":207.5,"over_th":166.7,"under_th":241.24,"over_am":"\u2212115","under_am":"\u2212112","pover":49,"call":"none","ev":-4.4,"edge":1.0},{"label":"Pass TDs","proj":1.41,"book":1.5,"over_th":1.19,"under_th":2.01,"over_am":"+187","under_am":"\u2212244","pover":50,"call":"OVER","ev":43.2,"edge":-0.1}],"td":{"pct":6.5,"fair":"16/1","book":"20/1","value":true}},{"name":"Bijan Robinson","pos":"RB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Rush yards","proj":72.04,"book":75.0,"over_th":57.79,"under_th":71.38,"over_am":"\u2212115","under_am":"\u2212114","pover":43,"call":"UNDER","ev":7.8,"edge":-3.0},{"label":"Carries","proj":14.92,"book":17.5,"over_th":13.32,"under_th":15.54,"over_am":"\u2212135","under_am":"+105","pover":37,"call":"UNDER","ev":29.1,"edge":-2.6},{"label":"Rec yards","proj":38.51,"book":36.5,"over_th":30.63,"under_th":37.82,"over_am":"\u2212114","under_am":"\u2212114","pover":47,"call":"none","ev":-0.1,"edge":2.0},{"label":"Receptions","proj":4.48,"book":4.5,"over_th":4.04,"under_th":4.75,"over_am":"+101","under_am":"\u2212132","pov